# Password template for locked posts (posts whose frontmatter declares `secret: <name>`).
# Usage: copy this file to .env (already gitignored) and replace the values
# with your own strong passwords.
#   cp .env.example .env
# frontmatter `secret: demo` maps to the env var BLOG_SECRET_DEMO (name uppercased).
#
# Security conventions:
# - The password is the ciphertext's only line of defense; >=16 chars
#   recommended (<8 rejects the build, <16 warns);
# - CI/production: a declared secret without a matching variable fails the
#   build outright (fail-closed, never produces plaintext);
# - Local builds without configuration are not blocked: a temporary password
#   is randomly generated per secret name and printed in the build output
#   (different on every build, for local unlock self-testing only); no
#   exemption under CI (process.env.CI) — fixed passwords are configured in
#   the build environment: production builds run on Cloudflare Workers Builds
#   (Dashboard → Build → Variables and Secrets), manual deploy.yml dispatches
#   use GitHub Secrets; without configuration the build fails;
# - Rotating a password = rewrite the post frontmatter's secret name or the
#   variable value, then rebuild (the salt is random on every build and the
#   ciphertext updates accordingly; previously published ciphertext takes
#   effect only after redeploying).

# Example: a post whose frontmatter declares `secret: couple` gets:
# BLOG_SECRET_COUPLE=a-long-passphrase-you-can-remember
